## a b s t r a c t
Transition metal carbon composites derived from metal organic frameworks (MOFs) attract increasing attention in microwave absorption field. However, finding a relatively facile and green method to prepare MOFs precursor is still a challenge. Besides, it is also difficult to obtain carbon nanotubes based compounds by only using MOF as sacrificed template. Herein, nickel (Ni) MOF is fabricated at room temperature with water as solvent. Afterwards, nickel/carbon nanotubes composite $({\mathsf{N i}}/{\mathsf{C N}})$ is prepared via only in-situ pyrolysis of Ni MOF. The pyrolysis temperature greatly affects nitrogen (N) dopant state for Ni/CN composites. The $\mathsf{N i}/\mathsf{C N}$ composite prepared at $700^{\circ}\mathsf C$ (Ni/CN-700) exhibits the maximum reflection loss (RL) of 65 dB with the effective absorbing bandwidth (EAB) about $4.6\mathsf{G H z}$ at $1.9\ \mathrm{mm}$ , when the filling loading is only $10\mathrm{\Deltawt\%}$ in the matrix. Remarkably, the Ni/CN composite is excellent microwave absorber with lightweight and strong absorption. The magnetic metal/carbon nanotubes derived from MOF prepared in green solvent offers a facile, environmentally friendly and designable strategy for exploring excellent microwave absorber.
