## 1. Introduction
Nowadays, electromagnetic (EM) pollution is affecting the environment and human health due to the extensive use of EM equipment and the rapid development of the communications industry. It is urgent to develop high-performance EM absorber for attenuating the EM waves. In general, microwave absorbing materials (MAMs) are expected to meet the following four conditions: strong absorbing ability, wide effective absorbing bandwidth, lightweight, and thin thickness [1–3]. Tremendous efforts have been made to constructing high efficient MAMs [4–6].
Recently, MAMs contained dual loss (magnetic loss and dielectric loss) mechanism have been widely studied [7–9]. The EM waves dissipation ability of magnetic materials can be improved by combining them with carbon materials. Utilizing metal organic frameworks (MOFs) as self-sacrificing template to fabricate dual loss absorber is drawing increasing attention. MOF is a coordination compound that consists of metal ion or its cluster as coordination connection center and organic molecule as ligand [10]. Materials derived from MOFs exhibit highly adjustable microstructure and component, which is attracting sustainable attention in not only microwave absorption [11,12] but also other fields [13– 16]. A series of MOFs, such as Fe-MIL-88A [17], ZIF-67 [18], Prussian blue [19] and so on, have been designed to fabricate transition metal/carbon composites. MOF derived magnetic carbon hybrids display favorable microwave absorption properties, but the synthesis process of precursors is always complicated and the synthetic solvents are usually organic. Therefore, finding a simple approach with green solvent to manufacture MOFs is significant for preparing MAMs.
Carbon nanotube is a potential material for EM wave absorption field due to the hollow structure and high conductivity [20–22]. Recently, combining carbon nanotubes with transition metal especially metal encapsulated within carbon nanotube as MAMs is widely investigated. Zhang et al. [23] fabricated graphene/ nitrogen-doped carbon nanotubes/CoNi alloys ternary complex that possessed the maximum RL of 44.23 dB with an EAB of $3.32~\mathrm{GHz}$ at $2.0\ \mathrm{mm}$ . Xu et al. [24] constructed CoNi alloys@nitrogen carbon nanotubes three dimensional carbon network by pyrolyzing the melamine and FeNi-MIL-88B that displayed strong absorption of $-47.0$ dB at $2.3~\mathrm{mm}$ . Wang et al. [25] exploited ${\sf C o}/$ C nanosheet@carbon nanotubes composite by heating ZIF-67 nanosheets under $\mathsf{A r/H}_{2}$ flow. However, the growth of carbon nanotubes on the surface of MOFs derived materials often requires reductive gas [25,26] or the addition of auxiliary agents (dicyandiamide or melamine) [23,24,27,28] in the pyrolysis process. Thus, it is still a challenge to prepare transition metal and carbon nanotube composites by only using MOF as a self-sacrificing template without special pyrolysis atmosphere.
Herein, we fabricated dicyanamide-based Ni MOF as the single precursor to prepare Ni/CN composite. This type of Ni MOF can be easily obtained at room temperature with the solvent of water. Dicyanamide is one of important parts of raw materials to construct Ni MOF. It serves as nitrogen source and promotes the formation of carbon nanotubes [29]. The as-prepared nitrogen rich Ni MOF is used as template to fabricate Ni/CN composite with uniform nitrogen distribution. The nickel ions are converted into nickel metal nanoparticles that promote the formation of graphitized carbon and carbon nanotubes. The morphology, composition, and EM parameters of hybrid are optimized by tuning the pyrolysis temperature. The intertwining carbon nanotubes forming conductive network contributes to the enhancement of conductive loss. The N element contributes to the defect/dipole polarization. The nickel nanoparticles contribute to magnetic loss. The optimal $\lvert\mathrm{Ni}\rvert$ CN composite exhibits excellent microwave absorption capacity with RL of $-65$ dB at $1.9\ \mathrm{mm}$ and the corresponding effective absorbing bandwidth is $4.6\ \mathrm{GHz}$ . Furthermore, the filler loading is only $10\mathrm{\:wt\%}$ . Therefore, this work provides a significant strategy for preparing lightweight, strong and efficient microwave absorbers.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Synthesis of Ni/CN microwave absorber
The synthetic process of Ni/CN composites is presented in Fig. 1. The Ni MOF precursor was prepared according to the modified method [30]. Firstly, $0.5\mathrm{~g~}$ sodium dicyanamide $(\mathsf{N a}(\mathsf{d c a}))$ and $0.24\ {\mathrm{g}}$ pyrazine $(\mathsf{C}_{4}\mathsf{H}_{4}\mathsf{N}_{2})$ ) were dissolved in $25~\mathrm{mL}$ water to form transparent solution. $_{0.815\mathrm{g}}$ nickel nitrate hexahydrate $(\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}.$ - - $6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\dot{}.$ ) was added into another $25~\mathrm{mL}$ water under vigorously stirring to obtain green transparent solution. Secondly, the above two solutions were mixed under vigorously stirring for $30\mathrm{min}$ at room temperature to form blue precipitate. The final product was washed with water several times and dried at $60~^{\circ}C$ overnight. Dca bridged the Ni atoms to form preliminary frameworks, and the pyrazine ligands linked them together to construct the final MOF. Finally, the above as-synthesized Ni MOF precursor powder was transferred into the corundum boat and heated at different temperatures $(600^{\circ}\mathsf C,700^{\circ}\mathsf C.$ , and $800^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ for $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ under Ar flowing. The obtained black composites were named as Ni/CN-600, Ni/CN700, and Ni/CN-800, respectively, according to different pyrolysis temperatures ( $600^{\circ}\mathsf C$ , $700^{\circ}\mathsf C$ and $800^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ . The raw materials and characterizations of the samples used in this work were described in the Supporting information.
## 3. Results and discussions
Fig. S1 shows the X-ray diffraction (XRD) pattern of Ni MOF precursor demonstrating its good crystallinity. The morphologies of Ni MOF precursor and $\mathsf{N i}/\mathsf{C N}$ composites are illustrated via scanning electron microscopy (SEM), as shown in Fig. 2. The obtained Ni MOF displays the sphere morphology assembled with some nanorods (Fig. 2(a)). The as-prepared $\mathsf{N i}/\mathsf{C N}$ composites $(\mathrm{Ni}/\mathrm{CN}{-}600,\mathrm{Ni}/$ CN-700, and Ni/CN-800) depict a similar morphology (Fig. 2(bd)). As illustrated in the inserted large magnification of SEM images, the Ni/CN-600 composite displays irregular structure with nanoparticles and short carbon nanotubes. When the pyrolysis temperature increases to more than $700^{\circ}\mathsf C,$ the carbon nanotubes become longer and connect together to form interwoven threedimensional network.
Fig. 3 depicts the transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images of $\mathsf{N i}/\mathsf{C N}$ composites. Notably, the formed carbon nanotubes are short and irregular (Fig. 3(a)) when the pyrolysis temperature is $600~^{\circ}\mathsf C$ , whereas after increasing the temperature to $700^{\circ}\mathsf C$ and $800^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ , carbon nanotubes present a bamboo-like shape with the tips containing Ni nanoparticles (Fig. 3(b and c)). The high-resolution TEM images further confirm that the Ni metal nanoparticles are embedded in graphitic carbon shell from ligands carbonization (Fig. 3(d-f)). Moreover, interplanar spacing of $0.2\ \mathrm{nm}$ corresponds to (1 1 1) plane of Ni metal. It is worth noting that further increasing the pyrolysis temperature to $900^{\circ}\mathsf C$ (Fig. S2(a, b)), the Ni/CN composite still displays the similar morphology with Ni/CN-700 composite. Considering the aspect of energy consumption, one expects that Ni/CN-700 composite will possess comparable microwave absorption properties to $\mathsf{N i}/\mathsf{C N}$ composites obtained at higher temperatures. Furthermore, this synthetic method can be spread to other transition metal based MOFs such as cobalt MOF (Fig. S2(c)). Moreover, the length and diameter of carbon nanotubes also can be optimized by adjusting the proportion of mixed metals. For example, as shown in Fig. S2(d), the carbon nanotubes derived from bimetallic NiCo MOF present larger diameters and longer lengths than Ni/CN composites.
The XRD patterns of $\mathsf{N i}/\mathsf{C N}$ composites derived from Ni MOF precursor are investigated in Fig. 4. As for the Ni/CN-600 composite, two weak diffraction peaks at approximate $41^{\circ}$ and $47^{\circ}$ match well with crystalline carbon (JCPDS No. 80–0004) [31]. When the pyrolysis temperature rises to $700~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ theses peaks disappear. Moreover, the peaks centered at about $2\uptheta\ =\ 44.5^{\circ}$ , $51.8^{\circ}$ , and $76.4^{\circ}$ refer to the (1 1 1), (2 0 0), and (2 2 0) planes of metallic nickel (JCPDS No. 04-0805). It is worth noting that the intensity of diffraction peaks gradually become stronger with the increase of pyrolysis temperature, indicating that the increased pyrolysis temperature is conductive to obtaining high crystalline composite. The broad diffraction peak located at about $26^{\circ}$ is the (0 0 2) plane of graphitic carbon (JCPDS No. 41-1487).
Raman spectroscopy was used to study the effect of pyrolysis temperature on the graphitization degree of composites. Fig. 5 depicts the Raman spectra of prepared Ni/CN composites at different temperatures. Distinctly, there are two broad characteristic peaks at approximately $1350~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ of D band and $1580~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ of G band. The former is associated with the disordered/defected carbon atom, and the latter denotes the in-plane vibration of $\mathsf{s p}^{2}$ carbon atoms [32]. The $\mathrm{I_{D}/I_{G}}$ values of $\mathrm{Ni}/\mathrm{CN-600,\mathrm{Ni}/C N-700}$ , and $\lvert\mathrm{Ni}\rvert$ CN-800 composites are 1.0061, 0.9897, and 0.9614, separately. As can be seen, with the increase of pyrolysis temperature, the $\mathrm{I_{D}/I_{G}}$ value decreases, indicating that the graphitization degree is gradually improved. The higher pyrolysis temperature is beneficial to improving the catalytic capacity of nickel nanoparticles, resulting in the further increase of graphitization degree for carbon. The high graphitization degree is helpful to increasing the conduction loss of MAMs.
The X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was used to analyze the elementary composition of $\mathsf{N i}/\mathsf{C N}$ composites. As displayed in Fig. 6(a), the wide scan spectra confirm the existence of C, N, and Ni elements in Ni/CN composites. The four fitting peaks in high-resolution C 1s spectrum centered at about $284.6\ \mathrm{~eV}$ 285 eV, 286 eV, and 289 eV belong to $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}-{\mathsf{C}}/{\mathsf{C}}={\mathsf{C}},\ {\mathsf{C}}-{\mathsf{N}},\ {\mathsf{C}}-0$ and $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ respectively (Fig. S3(a-c)) [32]. The CAN bond proves that
N atoms are successfully doped in the $\mathsf{N i}/\mathsf{C N}$ composites. From the $\mathsf{N i2p}$ spectrum (Fig. S3(d-f)), the two fitting characteristic peaks correspond to Ni $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ $(-854\mathrm{eV})$ , Ni $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ $({\sim}872~\mathrm{eV})$ indicating the existence of metallic Ni. Moreover, the two satellite peaks can be assigned to $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ [23,33]. Fig. 6(b-d) demonstrates that the N 1s spectrum includes pyridinic-N $(\sim399~\mathrm{eV})$ , pyrrolic-N $(\neg401\ \mathrm{{\eV})}$ , and graphitic-N $\cdot402\ \mathrm{eV})$ [34]. The area percentages of graphitic N for Ni/CN-700 and Ni/CN-800 composites are higher than that of $\mathrm{\DeltaNi}/\$ CN-600 composite, proving that the increasing of pyrolysis temperature could result in more graphitic N incorporated in the carbon. The graphitic N is advantageous to improving the conduction loss, while other N varieties are beneficial to the dipolar relaxation loss [35]. Hence, the dielectric loss of Ni/CN-700 and Ni/CN-800 composites maybe originate from conduction loss.
The Cole-Cole curve is investigated to analyze the dielectric relaxation for composites. The relationship between e’ and e‘‘ follows Equation (1) ( $\dot{\varepsilon}_{s}$ and $\scriptstyle{\varepsilon_{\infty}}$ present the static permittivity and the relative permittivity, respectively) according to Debye theory [41]:
$$
\left(\boldsymbol{\varepsilon}^{\prime}-\frac{\varepsilon_{s}+\varepsilon_{\infty}}{2}\right)^{2}+\left(\boldsymbol{\varepsilon}^{\prime}\right)^{2}=\left(\frac{\varepsilon_{s}-\varepsilon_{\infty}}{2}\right)^{2}
$$
$2{\mathrm{-}}8.5\operatorname{GHz}$ and then keep stable in the remaining frequency range, demonstrating that both the ferromagnetic resonance and eddy current loss contributes to the magnetic loss. The ${\sf C}_{0}$ values of $\lvert\mathrm{Ni}\rvert$ CN-700 and Ni/CN-800 composites vary in $2{\cdot}18~\mathrm{GHz}$ , suggesting that ferromagnetic resonance causes the dissipation of magnetic energy.
The transmission line theory is utilized to test the EM wave absorption properties of $\mathsf{N i}/\mathsf{C N}$ composites. The RL was obtained by the following Formulas [21]:
$$
Z_{i n}=Z_{0}\sqrt{\frac{\mu}{\varepsilon}}t a n h\bigg[j\bigg(\frac{2\pi}{c}\bigg)\sqrt{\varepsilon\mu}f d\bigg]
$$
$$
R L=20l g\bigg|\frac{Z_{i n}-Z_{0}}{Z_{i n}+Z_{0}}\bigg|
$$
where $Z_{\mathrm{in}}$ represents the equivalent input resistance of the sample. $Z_{0}$ is $377\Omega$ . The $\upmu$ and e are the complex permeability and complex permittivity of the sample, and the c is equal to $3\times10^{8}\mathrm{m}/s.f$ is the frequency and d is the thickness of the specimen. Fig. 9 describes the two-dimensional (a-c) and three-dimensional (d-f) RL plots of $\mathsf{N i}/\mathsf{C N}$ composites in the thickness range of $1.5\mathrm{-}4.0\ \mathrm{mm}$ . In general, when the RL value is below $-10{\mathrm{~dB}}$ , about 90 percent of EM waves can be effectively absorbed and its corresponding frequency range is EAB. As shown in Fig. 9(a, d), the RL values of Ni/CN-600 with different thicknesses are always bigger than $-10$ dB, demonstrating that Ni/CN-600 composite can hardly dissipate EM waves at $2-$ 18 GHz. The maximum RL value of Ni/CN-700 is $-65$ dB at $13.28~\mathrm{GHz}$ with EAB of $4.6~\mathrm{GHz}$ and the corresponding thickness is $1.9\mathrm{mm}$ (Fig. 9(b, e)). Ni/CN-800 composite exhibits the maximum RL value of $-56$ dB at $1.4~\mathrm{mm}$ and the EAB is $2.8\mathsf{G H z}$ (Fig. 9(c, f)). Obviously, the pyrolysis temperature has great influence on the absorption property of composite. Generally, both RL value and corresponding EAB are important parameters for evaluating EM wave absorbers. Therefore, Ni/CN-700 composite with the strongest RL value and broadest EAB has the best microwave performance among the three composites. The recent work on the nickel based composites for microwave absorption is summarized in Table 1. In terms of filler loading, matching thickness, EAB, and maximum RL value, the Ni/CN-700 composite with $10\mathrm{\Deltawt\%}$ filling loading displays relatively better microwave absorbing performance.
Outstanding microwave absorber always needs to satisfy strong attenuation ability and/or good impedance matching. Fig. 10(a) illustrates the attenuation constant $({\mathfrak{a}})$ of Ni/CN composites according to the formula below [17]:
$$
\alpha=\frac{\pi f\sqrt{2}}{c}\sqrt{\left(\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}\mu^{\prime\prime}-\varepsilon^{\prime}\mu^{\prime}\right)+\sqrt{\left(\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}\mu^{\prime\prime}-\mu^{\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime}\right)^{2}+\left(\mu^{\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}+\mu^{\prime\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime}\right)^{2}}}
$$
The $\textsf{\textsf{a}}$ value is proportional to the ability of the absorber to attenuate EM wave and can be calculated by Formula (4). The $\lvert\mathrm{Ni}\rvert$ CN-800 composite exhibits the largest $\textsf{\textsf{a}}$ values among the composites in the frequency range of $2{\cdot}18~\mathrm{GHz}$ . However, the microwave absorption performance of Ni/CN-800 composite is slightly worse than that of Ni/CN-700 composite (Fig. 9). Thus, the excellent microwave absorption ability of Ni/CN-700 composite might be mainly attributed to the impedance matching characteristic. When the surface resistance of absorber is equal to or close to the free space, the incident EM wave can achieve zero reflection on the surface of the material. A delta-function method is proposed to evaluate the impedance matching, which is expressed by the following formulas [47,51]:
$$
|\Delta|=|\sinh^{2}(K f d)-M|
$$
$$
\mathrm{K}={\frac{4\pi{\sqrt{\mu^{\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime}}}\sin{\frac{\delta_{\varepsilon}+\delta_{\mu}}{2}}}{\cos\delta_{\varepsilon}\cos\delta_{\mu}}}
$$
$$
\mathbf{M}={\frac{4\mu^{\prime}\cos\delta_{\varepsilon}\varepsilon^{\prime}\cos\delta_{\mu}}{{\bigl(}\mu^{\prime}\cos\delta_{\varepsilon}-\varepsilon^{\prime}\cos\delta_{\mu}{\bigr)}^{2}+{\Bigl[}\mathbf{tan}{\Bigl(}{\frac{\delta_{\mu}}{2}}-{\frac{\delta_{\varepsilon}}{2}}{\Bigr)}{\Bigr]}^{2}{\bigl(}\mu^{\prime}\cos\delta_{\varepsilon}+\varepsilon^{\prime}\cos\delta_{\mu}{\bigr)}^{2}}}
$$
The larger the area near small delta value $(|\Delta|\leq0.4)$ indicates the better impedance matching. Fig. 10(b-d) reveals the calculated $|\Delta|$ values of $\mathsf{N i}/\mathsf{C N}$ composites with different thicknesses of $1.5\textmd{-}$ $4.0\ \mathrm{mm}$ at 2–18 GHz. The Ni/CN-700 composite possesses the largest area of $|\Delta|\leq0.4$ (marked by the black dotted line) among the three samples. Therefore, the moderate attenuation ability and the best impedance matching endow the optimized microwave absorption properties of Ni/CN-700.
According to the above analysis results, the reason why the $\lvert\mathrm{Ni}\rvert$ CN-700 composite has the best EM wave absorption properties closely related to the following factors (Fig. 11). Firstly, 3D intertwined long carbon nanotubes not only provide migration and hopping paths for electrons but also ensure the fast electron transportation, resulting in the improved conductive loss [22]. Secondly, the doped N heteroatoms and defects in the carbon can be regarded as polarization center for induce dipole polarization [52,53]. Meanwhile, the interfacial polarization generated by multiple interfaces (carbon/paraffin, carbon/nickel, and nickel/carbon nanotube) in the composites are conducive to the conversion of EM energy into heat energy. Thirdly, the Ni nanoparticles provide magnetic loss by natural resonance and exchange resonance. Finally, the optimized impedance matching along with the appropriate attenuation ability ensure that the EM waves enter the absorber and are dissipated.
## 4. Conclusions
This work provides a convenient method to prepare N doped carbon nanotubes decorated with Ni nanoparticles via pyrolysing $N\mathrm{\Omega}$ -rich Ni MOF without additive. The organic linkers of dicyanamide and pyrazine served as the nitrogen source and carbon source. The $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ in Ni MOF is converted into metallic Ni and then promotes the formation of carbon nanotudes during thermal treatment process. Thanks to the synergetic interaction of the carbon nanotubes and Ni nanoparticles, the $\mathsf{N i/C N}$ composite demonstrates excellent microwave absorption ability. More strikingly, the microwave absorption properties of $\mathsf{N i}/\mathsf{C N}$ composite are easily ameliorated by the pyrolysis temperature. The $\mathsf{N i}/\mathsf{C N}$ composite obtained at $700~^{\circ}C$ is an excellent MAM, which has strong RL of $-65$ dB and wide EAB of $4.6\mathsf{G H z}$ at $1.9~\mathrm{mm}$ with filler loading of only $10\mathrm{\Deltawt\%}$ . This work supplies an effective approach to fabricate metallic nanoparticles with carbon nanotubes derived from MOF via a facile and green method.
